<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Hadramawt</h1><p class="st-meta">750 BCE – 300 CE (approx.) · capital Shabwa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The frankincense kingdom of south Arabia, ruling the Wadi Hadramawt from Shabwa and controlling the Dhofar groves and the port of Qana.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Hadramawt is named in Sabaean inscriptions of the 7th century BCE, when it was first an ally and then a rival of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-saba">Kingdom of Saba</a>. Its kings ruled the long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadi-hadhramaut">Wadi Hadramawt</a> and its tributaries from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shabwa">Shabwa</a>, a walled town where all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> had to be brought for taxing before caravans set off west.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its wealth came from frankincense, gathered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhofar">Dhofar</a> where Hadramawt founded the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumhuram">Sumhuram</a> (Khor Rori), and shipped through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qana-yemen">Qana</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> coast, a port described in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/periplus-of-the-erythraean-sea">Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</a>. The kingdom worshipped the moon god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayin">Sayin</a> and wrote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadramautic-language">Hadramitic</a>, a south Arabian language. It absorbed much of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-qataban">Kingdom of Qataban</a> in the 2nd century CE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the early 3rd century CE Saba sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shabwa">Shabwa</a>, and around 300 CE the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/himyarite-kingdom">Himyarite Kingdom</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shammar-yahrish">Shammar Yahrish</a> conquered Hadramawt outright. The name survived as a region, and the valley's later sultanates, such as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kathiri-sultanate">Kathiri Sultanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/quaiti-sultanate">Qu'aiti Sultanate</a>, inherited it.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 750 BCE – 300 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Arabia and the Gulf</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:44.78%;width:20.90%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>750 BCE – 300 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Shabwa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yada'il Bayyin, Ili'azz Yalit</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>South Arabian polytheism (Sayin)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hadramitic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Yemen</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hadhramaut, Hadramawt</dd></dl></aside></div>

